WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.069 --> 00:00:05.700
Claudine Kamikazi has a precious commodity
that's in high demand.

00:00:05.700 --> 00:00:11.959
She runs a "Mobile Solar Kiosk" at the central
bus station in the Rwandan capital, Kigali.

00:00:11.959 --> 00:00:17.730
Many of the phone users alighting here have
been on the road and off-line for hours.

00:00:17.730 --> 00:00:22.250
Claudine's kiosk enables them to charge up
their phones or surf the Net -- courtesy of

00:00:22.250 --> 00:00:25.599
solar power.

00:00:25.599 --> 00:00:34.239
"Business is good, I chose this because it's
something new.

00:00:34.239 --> 00:00:39.590
The fact that they always come to me shows
I made a good choice."

00:00:39.590 --> 00:00:44.710
The “Mobile Solar Kiosk” is the brainchild
of this man: Henri Nyakarundi.

00:00:44.710 --> 00:00:48.859
The idea was an obvious solution to the situation
on the ground, he says.

00:00:48.859 --> 00:00:54.180
70 percent of Rwanda's 11 and half a million
inhabitants have a mobile phone.

00:00:54.180 --> 00:00:57.199
But only 22 percent have electricity.

00:00:57.199 --> 00:00:59.969
"People spend a whole day here.

00:00:59.969 --> 00:01:03.480
There's no plug, if you look around, there
is no plug for people to charge.

00:01:03.480 --> 00:01:08.540
So it's a convenient way for people waiting
for the bus to charge their phone, pay a small

00:01:08.540 --> 00:01:10.930
fee and go on their business.

00:01:10.930 --> 00:01:15.659
/// Or whether we are in a rural area the
convenience stay the same.

00:01:15.659 --> 00:01:21.720
People don't have time to carry their charger
with them, to go to a store and ask somebody,

00:01:21.720 --> 00:01:23.869
now they can do that."

00:01:23.869 --> 00:01:28.440
It's been four years since Nyakarundi built
the very first "Mobile Solar Kiosk" with the

00:01:28.440 --> 00:01:30.130
assistance of a German company.

00:01:30.130 --> 00:01:36.520
There are now 40 spread across Rwanda – each
one managed by independent operators like

00:01:36.520 --> 00:01:37.520
Claudine.

00:01:37.520 --> 00:01:41.399
A portion of their earnings goes to the inventor.

00:01:41.399 --> 00:01:48.799
For him, what began as a simple idea has evolved
into far more than just a business model.

00:01:48.799 --> 00:01:57.479
"The social impact it's very key to our business.

00:01:57.479 --> 00:01:59.360
That's what we focus on.

00:01:59.360 --> 00:02:05.610
So we're mostly looking for women, or people
with a disability that don't have any job,

00:02:05.610 --> 00:02:13.140
no other opportunity but want to work.///
And it also brings a certain ... I wouldn´t

00:02:13.140 --> 00:02:16.760
say pride, but self-esteem."

00:02:16.760 --> 00:02:22.030
Demand is substantial in Rwanda -- both for
new jobs, and for access to electricity and

00:02:22.030 --> 00:02:27.370
the internet, which is why Henri Nyakarundi
sees huge market potential.

00:02:27.370 --> 00:02:32.790
By the end of this year he plans to expand
his pool of kiosks from 40 to between 600

00:02:32.790 --> 00:02:33.790
and 800.

00:02:33.790 --> 00:02:39.940
He also wants to take his invaluable experience
from Rwanda and introduce the successful micro-franchising

00:02:39.940 --> 00:02:44.450
model to neighboring Uganda -- where the majority
of the kiosk components are made.

00:02:44.450 --> 00:02:48.380
"It's not just creating jobs though micro-entrepreneurship.

00:02:48.380 --> 00:02:53.730
It's building this ecosystem that will support
them through that business.

00:02:53.730 --> 00:02:59.400
So we transport the kiosk to the location
for them, which is including all the fees.

00:02:59.400 --> 00:03:07.310
We give them support on the uniform they wear,
they have an app where they sell services."

00:03:07.310 --> 00:03:11.430
The Mugombwa area is a three-hour drive from
Kigali.

00:03:11.430 --> 00:03:16.100
Most people here get around on foot -- whether
to go shopping, pick up water, or to reach

00:03:16.100 --> 00:03:21.800
the nearest generator or electric socket.

00:03:21.800 --> 00:03:26.220
Emannuel Gakindi set up business with a "Solar
Kiosk" three years ago.

00:03:26.220 --> 00:03:29.660
It's the original edition, with no Internet
access.

00:03:29.660 --> 00:03:33.900
But people still come flocking wherever he
shows up.

00:03:33.900 --> 00:03:37.790
"Now I also have a paid assistant.

00:03:37.790 --> 00:03:45.280
And I can cover all my expenses from the phone-charging
business.”

00:03:45.280 --> 00:03:47.200
He's even added a second kiosk.

00:03:47.200 --> 00:03:52.140
He would also like to provide Internet access
to his customers -- which would require one

00:03:52.140 --> 00:03:56.220
of the new kiosks.

00:03:56.220 --> 00:04:01.370
Henri Nyakarundi is introducing the next generation
at the "Smart Africa" conference at the Kigali

00:04:01.370 --> 00:04:03.640
convention center.

00:04:03.640 --> 00:04:08.950
It brings together politicians with entrepreneurs,
donors and investors to discuss technical

00:04:08.950 --> 00:04:12.560
solutions for the continent and its people.

00:04:12.560 --> 00:04:16.570
Henri Nyakarundi is happy to see so much interest
in his innovation.

00:04:16.570 --> 00:04:21.570
"Our goal for the next ten years is 20 countries
in Africa.

00:04:21.570 --> 00:04:23.700
We've already selected the countries.

00:04:23.700 --> 00:04:29.880
We estimated 50- to a 100-thousand kiosks
in all those countries.

00:04:29.880 --> 00:04:33.600
That's about 50- to 100,000 micro-businesses
we'll be creating."

00:04:33.600 --> 00:04:39.310
A Mobile Solar Kiosk currently costs more
than two thousand dollars.

00:04:39.310 --> 00:04:45.100
Henri plans to maximize the number of components
made in Africa - lowering the price, and creating

00:04:45.100 --> 00:04:47.980
even more jobs.

00:04:47.980 --> 00:04:54.190
Claudine, the kiosk operator from the bus
station, has finished work for the day.

00:04:54.190 --> 00:04:58.980
Now she's helping her children finish their
homework.

00:04:58.980 --> 00:05:03.610
She can more or less determine her own working
hours -- and how much commission she earns

00:05:03.610 --> 00:05:07.620
from the sale of phone credits and electricity.

00:05:07.620 --> 00:05:15.380
"I can't afford to stay at home with my kids.

00:05:15.380 --> 00:05:18.740
I need to have an income.

00:05:18.740 --> 00:05:24.420
And the job helps me to make ends meet.

00:05:24.420 --> 00:05:30.590
It's made things a lot easier.”

00:05:30.590 --> 00:05:36.030
She earns around 100 dollars a month from
the kiosk, which in Rwanda amounts to double

00:05:36.030 --> 00:05:37.080
the average income.

